packets.rationale:
Item 4 (James, confirmed 2026-10-23): pick the best fixed fusion from the repo's own earlier commits, leak-free and online, 'persisted as a small calibration record, read by the answers'. Rankings are query-time and anything computed from history is a snapshot step, so the replay (EC.2-EC.5) writes a file and the answers only read it. The record needs:
- a version and a validity rule, so a record from another glia or another candidate set is never applied silently;
- one fallback rule every answer shares (blast_radius, diff_impact, review, pack, find --files);
- the replay's own numbers (each candidate's summed recall@10 and MRR, the online selection against the fallback, per test commit so a commit-cluster CI can be drawn), so a user and the release gate see what calibration measured on this repo.
The history snapshot (code-domain/src/snapshots.rs write_history / read_history) has the right discipline: data first, `meta.json` last with `data_hash`, an incomplete snapshot read as None. The record copies it. It lives in the engine, not in code-domain::snapshots: code-domain is hashed into PARSER_STAMP, and a ranking file format must never move the parse cache.
Watch: engine/src/watch.rs `durability` maps every `.glia/<x>` except `graph/` to High("glia_inputs"), which re-plans the walk; the calibration dir is no build input, so it maps to Low like `graph/`.

The measured selections (rank_eval.rs `round1_select`, `r3_select`) are defined over one replay protocol, and their gains hold only under it (rank_eval.rs main loop and `r3_tqueries`, at b3fc2ac7):
- commits oldest to newest; every signal of a test commit is read from strictly older commits; the test commit is folded into co-change and the text corpus AFTER its queries are scored;
- training-eligible: 1..=MAX_FILES (30) candidate files; test-eligible: 2..=30; the test commits are the newest `max_commits` (300) test-eligible ones;
- k1 (the single-seed shape): the commit's files sorted by h64("<sha>:<path>") (FNV-1a 64 then the splitmix64 finaliser `mix`), truncated to `max_seeds` (5), each one query whose targets are the commit's other files in file order; C0 = co[s][f] / (cnt[s] + 1);
- k2 / k3 / half (r3_tqueries shapes 0-2): the same hash order; seeds the first 2 (needs 3 files), 3 (needs 4) or n/2 (needs 4); targets the rest IN HASH ORDER; C0 = sum over seeds of co[s][f] / (cnt[s] + 1) accumulated in a BTreeMap;
- find (shape 3): no seed; the subject's text ranking's top R3_FIND_TOP (10) positive files (score desc, then file index) weighted score / total; C0x = sum over them of w * co[g][f] / (cnt[g] + 1); targets every changed file.
The text ranking T1s is BM25 over the FULL messages of older training commits with the subject line as the query (rank_eval `bm` vs `bm_s`): the replay needs each commit's message, which EB.1's messages.jsonl sidecar holds. The candidate file set is EB.3's `intent::text::Candidates` (rank_eval's index_graph rule: code MODULE files, sorted by path, with the file degree `deg`), so a tie that goes to the smaller file index goes to the smaller path, never to a NodeId. The engine already folds renames to current paths (engine/src/external/history.rs `commit_file_sets`, pub(crate)); the replay reuses it, a difference from rank_eval's `--no-renames` that EC.5's parity check bounds.

packets.implementation:
1. calibrate(root, merged, opts):
- merged.graphs.len() != 1 -> Err("calibration is per repo: got <n> repos");
- snapshot = code_domain::snapshots::read_history(root).ok_or("no history snapshot: run `glia history sync <repo>` first")?; messages = read_history_messages(root, &snapshot) (None is allowed: text candidates then score zeros and the summary says messages: false);
- cands = intent::text::candidates(merged, repo) (EB.3); specs = sets::specs(&opts.groups)?; shapes = union of the specs' shapes;
- replay = build_replay(&snapshot, messages.as_deref(), &cands, &opts.params, &shapes); state = ReplayState::new(merged, &cands, root, &replay) (EC.4's StructIndex and every registered StateHook);
- rows = run_sets(..); write_calibration(root, CalibrationMeta::new(snapshot.meta.head, snapshot.meta.data_hash, snapshot.meta.messages_hash), &Calibration { .. })?;
- summary rows: delta = selection_recall10 - fallback_recall10 and bootstrap_ci over the row's per_commit;
- `[calibrate] done surface=<s> sets=<n> test_commits=<t> ms=<ms>`.
No test commit -> a record with zero-query rows (select answers `no_queries`), Ok.

2. bootstrap_ci(per_commit): 2000 resamples of the test commits with replacement, a deterministic generator (rank_eval's Rng: wrapping add 0x9e37_79b9_7f4a_7c15 then `mix`, seed 0x5eed), each resample's delta = sum(sel_r10 - fb_r10) / sum(queries); the 2.5% / 97.5% order statistics. Zero commits -> (0.0, 0.0).

3. engine/tests/calibrate_step.rs (new): a generated repo (tempdir, GLIA_NO_PERSIST=1) of 12 Python modules in two import clusters plus one hub module every file imports; a synthetic history snapshot (write_history, no git, no messages) of 60 commits that change files of one cluster together; generate_one, then calibrate(..): the record is Loaded; SELh0 and SELs0 have rows k1 / k2 / k3 / half; the k1 row's queries equal the hand count of (test commits x min(5, files)); with co-change on the co-changed files, an H10 candidate is the SELh0 pick and the SELs0 pick is B0 (the hub draws PPR away from the cluster); selection_recall10 >= fallback_recall10 on both; bootstrap_ci is identical across two runs; no snapshot -> Err naming `glia history sync`; two runs write byte-identical calibration.json; a two-repo merge -> Err.

INTEGRATOR NOTE (0.5.3 merge): ONE file space (integration): every `Candidates` / `cands` in this packet is EB.3's per-repo view over EA.3's `glia_engine::rank::FileIndex` (rank_eval's index_graph rule, generated MODULE files dropped exactly as the harness drops them), and commit files map through `HistoryFile.p` with NO rename fold (EA.3 / the harness's `--no-renames`): `external::commit_file_sets` is not called. EC's verifier's tolerance for `the rename fold` and `EB.3's generated-file rule` is therefore gone: the replay's file space and C0 are the answer's. Split the step so the gate can run it in memory: `pub fn calibrate_snapshot(merged: &MergedGraph, root: &Path, snapshot: &HistorySnapshot, messages: Option<&[HistoryMessage]>, opts: &CalibrateOptions) -> Result<(Calibration, CalibrateSummary), String>` does everything but the reads and the write (root is read only for IDR's parent revisions, EC.12); `calibrate(root, merged, opts)` = read snapshot + messages, calibrate_snapshot, write_calibration. EG.6 / EG.7 call calibrate_snapshot with a snapshot built from rank_eval's own commit list. Parity tolerance (acceptance 2) tightens: with one file space and no rename fold the remaining differences are only the 2000-commit window and date vs topo order; record them.
